            <ArticleTitle>Considerations for Safe Innovation: The Case of Graphene.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>9574-9593</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acsnano.7b04120</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The terms &quot;Safe innovation&quot; and &quot;Safe(r)-by-design&quot; are currently popular in the field of nanotechnology. These terms are used to describe approaches that advocate the consideration of safety aspects already at an early stage of the innovation process of (nano)materials and nanoenabled products. Here, we investigate the possibilities of considering safety aspects during various stages of the innovation process of graphene, outlining what information is already available for assessing potential hazard, exposure, and risks. In addition, we recommend further steps to be taken by various stakeholders to promote the safe production and safe use of graphene.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
